   "text": "I read the structure in four pieces and joined them into one SMILES.\n\n- **Right-hand end:** a benzothiazole carrying a 2-hydroxypropan-2-yl group at C5 and an NH\u2013C(=O)\u2013(6-trifluoromethylpyridin-2-yl) amide at C6. Its C2 is bonded to a 1,4-disubstituted cyclohexane.\n- **Cyclohexane:** the wedge to the benzothiazole and the hashed CH2 on the opposite carbon indicate a trans arrangement. I wrote this as `[C@H]1CC[C@@H](...)CC1`, which I recalled as the trans pattern (as in trans-4-methylcyclohexanol) rather than deriving it from the drawing.\n- **Centre:** the cyclohexyl CH2 is bonded to the N of a 2-azaspiro[3.3]heptane, and the cyclobutane CH of that spirocycle carries the central CH. That CH bears two arms:\n  - a CH2\u2013NH arm leading to a 4-amino-isoindoline-1,3-dione whose ring N is attached to a 2,6-dioxopiperidin-3-yl group (the thalidomide-type end);\n  - a CH2\u2013N arm leading to a piperidine whose C4 is bonded to a phenyl ring.\n- **Lower right:** the phenyl ring is para-substituted with an NH bonded to a pyrazine. The pyrazine carries a carboxamide next to the NH carbon and a piperidin-1-yl group on the other side.\n\nThe spiro CH and the central CH are drawn with plain bonds, so they get no stereo marks. The 2,6-dioxopiperidin-3-yl CH is also drawn plain and gets none.\n\nSMILES: CC(C)(O)c1cc2nc(sc2cc1NC(=O)c1cccc(n1)C(F)(F)F)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](CN3CC4(C3)CC(C4)C(CNc3cccc5c3C(=O)N(C3CCC(=O)NC3=O)C5=O)CN3CCC(CC3)c3ccc(Nc5nc(N6CCCCC6)cnc5C(N)=O)cc3)CC1"
  }
